      "text": "Chapter 9: structure based drug design with active learning\n    - numpy does not have native GPU support, outputs to CPU, torch.to(device) for acceleration\n        - therefore libraries like cupy (numpy for GPU) and JAX (numpy for TPU) were maintained\n    - torch.manual_seed() abstracts hardware specific acceleraetion and source code applies seed to multi/single GPU\n    ? (3)multi-layer-perceptron is used but unclear if minimally effecitve MLP or simple for usecase from author\n\n    9.1: molecular docking\n        - consider active vs. allosteric sites: protein-ligand, protein-protein, and protein-DNA docking types\n        - minimal protein-ligand docking workflow: input, structure preparation, docking\n            - conformational sampling and pose evaluation are two main challenges\n            - scoring and ranking to account for: van der waals, electrostatics, hydrogen bonding, etc.\n            - poses are possible geometries\n        - 9.1.3: data preparation, 9.1.4: running the dock using a rigid protein, flexible ligand with a binding box (mdtraj from pdb)\n\n    9.2: active learning\n        -\n\n    9.3: generalization (hence drop-out 50% as opposed to 20% due to active learning?)\n        -",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Chapter 9: structure based drug design with active learning\n    - numpy does not have native GPU support, outputs to CPU, torch.to(device) for acceleration\n        - therefore libraries like cupy (numpy for GPU) and JAX (numpy for TPU) were maintained\n    - torch.manual_seed() abstracts hardware specific acceleraetion and source code applies seed to multi/single GPU\n    ? (3)multi-layer-perceptron is used but unclear if minimally effecitve MLP or simple for usecase from author\n\n    9.1: molecular docking\n        - consider active vs. allosteric sites: protein-ligand, protein-protein, and protein-DNA docking types\n        - minimal protein-ligand docking workflow: input, structure preparation, docking\n            - conformational sampling and pose evaluation are two main challenges\n            - scoring and ranking to account for: van der waals, electrostatics, hydrogen bonding, etc.\n            - poses are possible geometries\n        - 9.1.3: data preparation, 9.1.4: running the dock using a rigid protein, flexible ligand with a binding box (mdtraj from pdb)\n\n    9.2: active learning\n        -\n\n    9.3: generalization (hence drop-out 50% as opposed to 20% due to active learning?)\n        -",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "aK"
    },
