    {
      "id": "1JtrJV40sySf3RJcRmdnS",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -5481.415523830357,
      "y": -1481.9243566116347,
      "width": 632,
      "height": 1845,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": "yu7BNWqjVi6X6-zIkYTWG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1327561549,
      "version": 2,
      "versionNonce": 1792546985,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784685404189,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "- Frame the business problem as a measurable ML task first\n  - Pick the target variable and success metric before touching data — a mismatched metric (e.g. accuracy on imbalanced fraud data) invalidates everything downstream\n- Prevent data leakage at the source, not in review\n  - Time-based splits for temporal data, group-aware splits when rows share an entity (user/patient), and fit all scalers/encoders only on the training fold\n- Treat feature engineering as a versioned artifact\n  - A feature store (Feast, Tecton, or a simple point-in-time-correct table) keeps train/serve features consistent and prevents online/offline skew\n- EDA should produce falsifiable hypotheses, not just plots\n  - Check target leakage, class imbalance, missingness patterns (MCAR/MAR/MNAR), and distribution drift between train and expected production data\n- Choose eval metrics by problem type and cost asymmetry, not convention\n  - Precision/recall/F1 or PR-AUC for imbalanced classification, calibration (Brier score) when probabilities drive decisions, quantile loss for uncertainty-aware regression\n- Always beat a trivial baseline before trusting a complex model\n  - Majority class, linear/logistic regression, or a simple heuristic — if a gradient-boosted model barely beats it, the signal may not be there\n- Track every experiment with lineage: code version, data version, hyperparameters, metrics\n  - MLflow, Weights & Biases, or DVC — reproducibility is a debugging and audit requirement, not a nice-to-have\n- Version models and data together, not just code\n  - A model artifact without its training data hash and preprocessing pipeline version is not reproducible and not safely rollback-able\n- Test ML code like software: unit test transforms, contract-test the training pipeline, and add data validation (Great Expectations / pandera) as a CI gate\n- Monitor for drift after deployment, not just uptime\n  - Feature drift (PSI, KL divergence), prediction drift, and label/concept drift once ground truth arrives — alert on threshold breaches, not just crashes\n- Deploy with a rollback path: shadow mode first, then canary, then champion-challenger\n  - Shadow runs the new model silently alongside production to compare outputs before it ever affects a user\n- For LLM-based features, separate system instructions from user input explicitly to prevent prompt injection, and version prompts like code (diffable, reviewable, rollback-able)\n- RAG systems fail in specific, recurring ways: retrieval returns relevant-but-wrong-grain chunks, embeddings drift when the embedding model changes (requires full re-index), and context-stuffing degrades generation quality past a token budget — validate retrieval quality (recall@k) separately from generation quality\n- Agentic workflows need bounded iteration and explicit tool-call validation\n  - Cap retries/steps, validate tool outputs against a schema before feeding them back into the loop, and log every step for observability (Chain of Responsibility: retrieve → grade → generate → validate)\n- Fail fast and gracefully on LLM API limits\n  - Handle rate limits, timeouts, and context-window overflows with explicit fallback logic (smaller model, cached response, truncated context) rather than an unhandled exception reaching the user\n- Senior-level judgment: know when NOT to use ML\n  - A well-tuned heuristic or SQL rule is often more reliable, cheaper, and easier to debug than a model for problems with clear deterministic logic",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "- Frame the business problem as a measurable ML task first\n  - Pick the target variable and success metric before touching data — a mismatched metric (e.g. accuracy on imbalanced fraud data) invalidates everything downstream\n- Prevent data leakage at the source, not in review\n  - Time-based splits for temporal data, group-aware splits when rows share an entity (user/patient), and fit all scalers/encoders only on the training fold\n- Treat feature engineering as a versioned artifact\n  - A feature store (Feast, Tecton, or a simple point-in-time-correct table) keeps train/serve features consistent and prevents online/offline skew\n- EDA should produce falsifiable hypotheses, not just plots\n  - Check target leakage, class imbalance, missingness patterns (MCAR/MAR/MNAR), and distribution drift between train and expected production data\n- Choose eval metrics by problem type and cost asymmetry, not convention\n  - Precision/recall/F1 or PR-AUC for imbalanced classification, calibration (Brier score) when probabilities drive decisions, quantile loss for uncertainty-aware regression\n- Always beat a trivial baseline before trusting a complex model\n  - Majority class, linear/logistic regression, or a simple heuristic — if a gradient-boosted model barely beats it, the signal may not be there\n- Track every experiment with lineage: code version, data version, hyperparameters, metrics\n  - MLflow, Weights & Biases, or DVC — reproducibility is a debugging and audit requirement, not a nice-to-have\n- Version models and data together, not just code\n  - A model artifact without its training data hash and preprocessing pipeline version is not reproducible and not safely rollback-able\n- Test ML code like software: unit test transforms, contract-test the training pipeline, and add data validation (Great Expectations / pandera) as a CI gate\n- Monitor for drift after deployment, not just uptime\n  - Feature drift (PSI, KL divergence), prediction drift, and label/concept drift once ground truth arrives — alert on threshold breaches, not just crashes\n- Deploy with a rollback path: shadow mode first, then canary, then champion-challenger\n  - Shadow runs the new model silently alongside production to compare outputs before it ever affects a user\n- For LLM-based features, separate system instructions from user input explicitly to prevent prompt injection, and version prompts like code (diffable, reviewable, rollback-able)\n- RAG systems fail in specific, recurring ways: retrieval returns relevant-but-wrong-grain chunks, embeddings drift when the embedding model changes (requires full re-index), and context-stuffing degrades generation quality past a token budget — validate retrieval quality (recall@k) separately from generation quality\n- Agentic workflows need bounded iteration and explicit tool-call validation\n  - Cap retries/steps, validate tool outputs against a schema before feeding them back into the loop, and log every step for observability (Chain of Responsibility: retrieve → grade → generate → validate)\n- Fail fast and gracefully on LLM API limits\n  - Handle rate limits, timeouts, and context-window overflows with explicit fallback logic (smaller model, cached response, truncated context) rather than an unhandled exception reaching the user\n- Senior-level judgment: know when NOT to use ML\n  - A well-tuned heuristic or SQL rule is often more reliable, cheaper, and easier to debug than a model for problems with clear deterministic logic",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "aQ"
    },
